<Layout title="the mil-tok" description="the bixel 1000: the master lexicon of kahu-tok, written by bixel in four editions, one per founder language. this is the english edition.">
  <article class="entry-body lexicon">
    <p class="lexicon-eyebrow"><a class="wiki-link" href="/language">language</a> / the mil-tok</p>
    <h1>the mil-tok</h1>
    <p class="entry-primary-source">held in the kahu archive. a primary document, in the voice of its maker. the encyclopedia's own entries begin <a class="wiki-link" href="/language/kahu-tok">here</a>.</p>
    <p class="lede">the thousand words. the master lexicon of <a class="wiki-link" href="/language/kahu-tok">kahu-tok</a>, set down by <a class="wiki-link" href="/people/bixel">bixel</a> in the <a class="wiki-link" href="/eras/age-of-tapestry">age of tapestry</a>, for a people learning to speak as one.</p>
    <hr class="kahu-rule" />

    <section class="lexicon-front">
      <p>i did not make a language from nothing. i listened to what people were already saying to one another across the fences of the <a class="wiki-link" href="/places/nu-tera">nu-tera</a>: the <span class="kt">tok-peles</span> of the mountain bunkers, the spanish of the hearth and the kitchen, the mandarin of the mountain and the shared work, the russian of the ice and the winter and the bath, and the old sacred words the language already carried, <span class="kt">kahu</span> among them. i found the words that were winning. i fixed the spelling, mended the worst tangles, and wrote it down.</p>

      <p>my brief was simple and hard. a tongue a grieving adult could learn in one winter. a tongue no founder group could claim as theirs alone. and a tongue that, by its own shape, could not say certain things.</p>

      <p>i wrote the mil-tok four times. once in spanish, once in russian, once in mandarin, and once in english, so that each people could come to <span class="kt"><a class="wiki-link" href="/language/kahu-tok">kahu-tok</a></span> through the tongue they already grieved in. the four are one work in four mouths; none is the translation of another. what you read here is the english edition. by english i mean my english, the tongue the bunker years left us, which is not the english of the old world: it had already taken in the sacred words the pacific people carried, <span class="kt">kahu</span> and <span class="kt">aroa</span> and <span class="kt">mana</span> among them, generations before i set them down. the <span class="kt">tok-peles</span> of the mountain bunkers and the pacific sacred register are two branches of that one english, and i wrote them both as what they were to me: english.</p>

      <p class="lexicon-margin">i wrote it four times. the russian first, because a maker must stand somewhere.</p>

      <h2>how the language is built</h2>
      <p><strong>importance first, everywhere.</strong> a sentence leads with what matters most, not with a fixed subject. so do compounds. <span class="kt">peli-shen</span> leads with the danger; <span class="kt">klimb-long</span> leads with the climb. there is no head but the heart of the thing. the name of the world is the lesson: <span class="proper">tora vyl drak</span>, hope, call, last, the hope of the last call.</p>
      <p><strong>words traveled with the people who did the work.</strong> the hearth words are spanish, the mountain and the labor words are mandarin, the ice and winter and travel words are russian, and the everyday base is english in its two branches: the <span class="kt">tok-peles</span> creole of the mountain bunkers for daily speech, and the older pacific register, english too, for the sacred words. people share fires, so the strata bleed; but you can still hear, in any sentence, where its speakers came from.</p>
      <p><strong>phonemic spelling, one case, no silent letters.</strong> five vowels, written as heard. proper names are underlined. the mark <span class="kt">!</span> throws to the front of a sentence the one thing that must be heard first.</p>
      <p><strong>roughly three hundred and fifty roots; the rest is built.</strong> compounds and doubling do the work. doubling is intensity or repetition: <span class="kt">tok-tok</span> is conversation, <span class="kt">think-think</span> is to worry, <span class="kt">zou-zou</span> is to wander.</p>

      <h2>what the language will not say</h2>
      <p>the mil-tok has no word for money. none for debt, for rent, for interest. it has <span class="kt">hav</span>, to hold and to carry and to have-with-you, and it has no word for <em>own</em>. exchange is <span class="kt">trok</span>, barter, and <span class="kt">giv-giv</span>, sharing. wealth is <span class="kt">koru-hav</span>, what is held for all. poverty is <span class="kt">sin-hav</span>, without-holding.</p>
      <p>this is not the accident of a small lexicon. it is the lexicon\u2019s argument. a people who survive only together should not be able to say, easily, that one person\u2019s having is set against another\u2019s. the language does not give them the words to make that case. to stand outside the <a class="wiki-link" href="/concepts/kahu">kahu</a>, you would have to stand outside the language as well.</p>
    </section>

    {groups.map((g) => (
      <section class="lexicon-section">
        <h2 class="lexicon-h"><span class="lexicon-letter">{g.letter}</span> {g.title}</h2>
        {g.intro && <p class="lexicon-intro">{g.intro}</p>}
        <dl class="lexicon-list">
          {g.words.map((word) => (
            <div class="lexicon-row">
              <dt class="kt">{word.w}</dt>
              <dd>
                {word.d}
                {word.note && <span class="lexicon-note"> {word.note}</span>}
              </dd>
            </div>
          ))}
        </dl>
      </section>
    ))}

    <hr class="kahu-rule" />
    <section class="lexicon-coda">
      <h2>a note on the count</h2>
      <p>the mil-tok is a thousand words. some ninety are the old shared roots i only regularized; the rest are built on the principles above, three hundred and fifty working roots and the compounds, doublings, and set phrases that grow from them, the way any living contact tongue grows. this page shows the working spine of it, the roots and the compounds a learner meets first. the full thousand lives in the speaking.</p>
      <p>the language will go on without me. words will enter it that i never set down, taken from names and weather and work i will not be here to see, and the compounds will breed compounds until the speaking has run far past this page. that is as it should be. i did not build a finished thing. i built a beginning that could carry its own growing. a language no one can add to is a language no one is still speaking, and i would rather leave one that outruns me than one that holds still.</p>
      <p class="lexicon-sign"><span class="proper"><a class="wiki-link" href="/people/bixel">bixel</a></span>, the tongue</p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>

